course: dictionary
lesson: 10428
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Troops can mean soldiers, a group of animals, or a way of walking together. Look at the words around it to find the right meaning.
explain: «On 6 June 1944, D-Day, Allied troops landed on the beaches of Normandy in France.» Ask yourself who is doing the action. Soldiers landed from the sea, so troops means soldiers here.
explain: «Squirrel monkeys travel in big troops.» Monkeys are animals, not soldiers, so a troop here is a group of animals that live together. Troop can also be a verb: «the children trooped into the hall» means they walked in together as a group.
fact: Troops means soldiers, especially a large group of them.
fact: A troop is a group of soldiers, scouts, or animals such as monkeys.
fact: To troop somewhere means to walk there together as a group, often slowly or tiredly.
fact: «A troop of Girl Scouts» and «a troop of baboons» both use troop to mean a group.
fact: Useful phrases: send in the troops, troop in, troop out.
exercise: «On 6 June 1944, Allied troops landed on the beaches of Normandy.» What does troops mean here? | Monkeys living in trees | *Soldiers | Children walking into assembly | Scouts on a camping trip
exercise: «Squirrel monkeys travel in big troops.» What is a troop here? | A large map of the forest | *A group of animals that live together | A group of soldiers marching to war | A tired walk home
exercise: «The children trooped into the hall.» What does trooped mean? | Shouted loudly | Ran out alone | Sat down quietly | *Walked in together as a group
test: «A troop of Girl Scouts went camping by the lake.» What is a troop here? | A group of soldiers | *A group of scouts | A lake | A single scout
test: Which phrase means soldiers are being sent somewhere? | *Send in the troops | Send in the letters | Send in the cat | Send in the dinner
exam: Which sentence uses troops to mean soldiers? | The monkeys shared a banana in the trees | *The army sent troops to help the town | We sang a song in assembly | Our class trooped out to lunch
